From 7717768d10c25739a904d1e15386bcb0e1e00d17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:33:14 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 39/48] script: add OP_CHECKMLDSA44 for ML-DSA-44 checks in
 P2MR leaves

Byte 0xf0, OP_SUCCESS240 in BIP 342, executes as OP_CHECKMLDSA44 in a
P2MR leaf that is executed (leaf version 0xc0, depth one or more) when
the new SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_MLDSA44 flag is set. Everywhere else the byte
keeps its meaning: a bad opcode in legacy and witness v0 scripts, and
OP_SUCCESS240 in P2TR leaves and without the flag. No deployment sets
the flag yet.

The opcode takes an ML-DSA-44 public key in three chunks (520, 520 and
272 bytes) and a signature in five (four of 520 bytes, then 340, or 341
with an explicit hash type), so MAX_SCRIPT_ELEMENT_SIZE does not change.
The checks run in this order, as EvalChecksigTapscript runs those of a
BIP 342 signature: at least eight stack elements; a signature with any
non-empty chunk uses 250 units of validation weight; the public key
chunk sizes; five empty chunks push false; the signature chunk sizes;
the hash type, with the rules of BIP 341. The message is the BIP 341
signature message of a tapscript spend with key_version 1, so it never
equals that of a BIP 342 signature, and the check is FIPS 204
ML-DSA.Verify (pure, external interface) with the context string
"P2MR/MLDSA44/EXP0". An invalid signature fails the script; a valid one
pushes 1.

- SignatureHashSchnorr takes the key version, 0 unless given, so every
  existing caller computes the same message as before.
- The checkers gain CheckMLDSA44Signature: the hash type and the message
  in GenericTransactionSignatureChecker, the verification in the virtual
  VerifyMLDSA44Signature, which the caching checker overrides with cache
  entries under their own padding, 'M'. DeferringSignatureChecker passes
  the new check through.
- Four new script errors, OP_CHECKMLDSA44 as an opcode name, and the
  flag name P2MR_MLDSA44.

See doc/p2mr-mldsa44.md, added later in this series.
